<body>
<div class="report-container">

<h1>Lichess Chess Game Analytics:<br>Patterns of Victory, Openings, and Player Dynamics</h1>
<p class="subtitle">A comprehensive analysis of 20,058 Lichess chess games — quantifying the first-move advantage, rating dynamics, opening effectiveness, and time control patterns. Generated May 7, 2026.</p>

<!-- TOC -->
<div class="toc">
  <strong>Contents</strong>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#s1">Dataset Overview &amp; Game Distribution</a></li>
    <li><a href="#s2">White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</a></li>
    <li><a href="#s3">Rating Dynamics and Their Impact on Outcomes</a></li>
    <li><a href="#s4">Opening Analysis: Popular Openings and Their Success Rates</a></li>
    <li><a href="#s5">Time Control Patterns and Game Characteristics</a></li>
    <li><a href="#s6">Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</a></li>
  </ol>
</div>

<!-- KPI Dashboard -->
<div class="kpi-grid">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">20,058</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Total Games Analyzed</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">52.3%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">White Win Rate (Decisive)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">4.7%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Draw Rate</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">80.5%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Games Rated</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">59.7</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg. Turns per Game</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 1: Dataset Overview -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<h2 id="s1">1. Dataset Overview &amp; Game Distribution</h2>

<p>The dataset comprises <strong>20,058 chess games</strong> collected from Lichess.org. Of these, <strong>16,155 (80.5%)</strong> were rated games and <strong>3,903 (19.5%)</strong> were casual/unrated. Draws accounted for <strong>950 games (4.7%)</strong>, leaving <strong>19,108 decisive games</strong> for win-rate analysis.</p>

<div class="flex-col">
<div class="chart-container">
  <canvas id="chartVictoryStatus"></canvas>
</div>
<div class="chart-container">
  <canvas id="chartWinnerDist"></canvas>
</div>
</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>Outcome</th><th class="num">Games</th><th class="num">Share of Total</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Resignation</td><td class="num">11,147</td><td class="num">55.6%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Checkmate</td><td class="num">6,325</td><td class="num">31.5%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Time Expiration</td><td class="num">1,680</td><td class="num">8.4%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Draw</td><td class="num">906</td><td class="num">4.5%</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="stat-box">
  <strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Resignation is the dominant endgame mechanism (55.6%), reflecting Lichess's culture of players conceding lost positions rather than playing to mate. Only 31.5% of games reach actual checkmate. With draws at just 4.7%, the dataset skews toward decisive outcomes — far lower draw rates than elite classical chess (~30–40%).
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 2: White Advantage (CENTERPIECE) -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<h2 id="s2">2. White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</h2>

<p>Of the 19,108 decisive games, <strong>white won 10,001 (52.3%)</strong> and black won 9,107 (47.7%). This 4.7 percentage-point gap confirms a statistically meaningful first-move advantage — white wins approximately <strong>1.10 games for every 1 game black wins</strong>.</p>

<div class="chart-container">
  <canvas id="chartWhiteEdge"></canvas>
</div>

<h3>2.1 White Win Rate by Victory Status</h3>

<table>
  <tr><th>Victory Status</th><th class="num">White Wins</th><th class="num">Total Decisive</th><th class="num">White Win %</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Checkmate</td><td class="num">3,344</td><td class="num">6,325</td><td class="num highlight">52.9%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Resignation</td><td class="num">5,844</td><td class="num">11,147</td><td class="num highlight">52.4%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Time Expiration</td><td class="num">813</td><td class="num">1,636</td><td class="num">49.7%</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="stat-box">
  <strong>Notable:</strong> Time expiration is the <em>only</em> category where white's edge disappears (49.7%). This likely reflects black's defensive pragmatism in time-scramble positions — protecting their clock while forcing white to prove an advantage. In classical over-the-board chess, white's win rate under time pressure would be higher; online blitz equalizes this dimension.
</div>

<h3>2.2 White Advantage by Rating Bracket</h3>

<div class="chart-container">
  <canvas id="chartWhiteByRating"></canvas>
</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>Rating Bracket</th><th class="num">White Wins</th><th class="num">Total Decisive</th><th class="num">White Win %</th></tr>
  <tr><td>&lt;1,200</td><td class="num">546</td><td class="num">1,529</td><td class="num">35.7%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>1,200 – 1,500</td><td class="num">2,687</td><td class="num">5,548</td><td class="num">48.4%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>1,500 – 1,800</td><td class="num">4,030</td><td class="num">7,468</td><td class="num highlight">54.0%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>1,800 – 2,100</td><td class="num">2,014</td><td class="num">3,562</td><td class="num highlight">56.5%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2,100+</td><td class="num">724</td><td class="num">1,001</td><td class="num highlight">72.3%</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="stat-box">
  <strong>Critical finding:</strong> White's advantage scales dramatically with rating. At the sub-1200 level, white actually <em>underperforms</em> (35.7%), suggesting beginners do not know how to convert the initiative. By 1500+, white's edge stabilizes at 54%+. At the 2100+ elite level, white wins nearly 3 out of every 4 decisive games (72.3%) — indicating master-level players routinely convert the first-move advantage into a win. This is the largest effect size in the entire study.
</div>

<h3>2.3 White Win Rate by Time Control</h3>

<div class="chart-container">
  <canvas id="chartWhiteByTC"></canvas>
</div>

<p>Across the 10 most common time controls, white's win rate clusters tightly around 51–53%, with one notable outlier: <strong>15+10</strong> (55.3%) and <strong>8+0</strong> (53.1%) show the strongest white advantages. Time controls with increment (the "+N" component) tend to favor white slightly more, as increment benefits the player with the initiative who can generate threats without flag concerns.</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 3: Rating Dynamics -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<h2 id="s3">3. Rating Dynamics and Their Impact on Outcomes</h2>

<h3>3.1 Rating Distribution</h3>

<p>The pooled rating distribution (combining white and black players) centers around a mean of <strong>~1,593</strong> (median 1,565), with a standard deviation of 291 Elo points. The distribution peaks in the 1,400–1,600 range, consistent with Lichess's typical online player base.</p>

<div class="chart-container">
  <canvas id="chartRatingDist"></canvas>
</div>

<h3>3.2 Rating Differential vs. Win Probability</h3>

<p>The Pearson correlation between rating differential (white – black) and white winning is <strong>r = 0.367</strong> — a moderate but robust effect. More instructive is the binned analysis below, which shows precisely how win probability shifts across rating gaps.</p>

<div class="chart-container">
  <canvas id="chartRatingDiff"></canvas>
</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>White Rating – Black Rating</th><th class="num">White Wins</th><th class="num">Games</th><th class="num">White Win %</th></tr>
  <tr><td>&lt; –400</td><td class="num">154</td><td class="num">967</td><td class="num">15.9%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>–400 to –200</td><td class="num">485</td><td class="num">1,978</td><td class="num">24.5%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>–200 to –100</td><td class="num">757</td><td class="num">2,089</td><td class="num">36.2%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>–100 to 0</td><td class="num">2,076</td><td class="num">4,356</td><td class="num">47.7%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>0 to +100</td><td class="num">2,419</td><td class="num">4,292</td><td class="num highlight">56.4%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>+100 to +200</td><td class="num">1,525</td><td class="num">2,274</td><td class="num">67.1%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>+200 to +400</td><td class="num">1,622</td><td class="num">2,061</td><td class="num">78.7%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>&gt; +400</td><td class="num">963</td><td class="num">1,091</td><td class="num highlight">88.3%</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>3.3 Higher-Rated Player Win Probability (Absolute Differential)</h3>

<p>When we look at the <em>higher-rated</em> player regardless of color, the effect of rating becomes even clearer:</p>

<div class="chart-container">
  <canvas id="chartFavoredWin"></canvas>
</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>Rating Gap (Absolute)</th><th class="num">Higher-Rated Wins</th><th class="num">Games</th><th class="num">Win Rate</th></tr>
  <tr><td>0 – 25</td><td class="num">1,451</td><td class="num">2,811</td><td class="num">51.6%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>25 – 50</td><td class="num">1,125</td><td class="num">2,181</td><td class="num">51.6%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>50 – 100</td><td class="num">2,043</td><td class="num">3,490</td><td class="num">58.5%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>100 – 150</td><td class="num">1,624</td><td class="num">2,523</td><td class="num">64.4%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>150 – 200</td><td class="num">1,230</td><td class="num">1,832</td><td class="num">67.1%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>200 – 300</td><td class="num">1,917</td><td class="num">2,552</td><td class="num">75.1%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>300 – 400</td><td class="num">1,188</td><td class="num">1,474</td><td class="num">80.6%</td></tr>
  <tr><td>400+</td><td class="num">1,774</td><td class="num">2,055</td><td class="num highlight">86.3%</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="stat-box">
  <strong>Interpretation:</strong> For rating gaps under 50 points, the higher-rated player wins only ~51.6% of the time — essentially a coin flip. Meaningful predictive power emerges at the 50-point gap (58.5%), and by a 200-point gap the higher-rated player wins 3 out of 4 games. At 400+ points, it becomes a near-certainty (86.3%). This provides a practical benchmark: a 100-point rating advantage translates to roughly a 2:1 win probability edge.
</div>

<h3>3.4 Average Game Length by Rating Tier</h3>

<div class="chart-container">
  <canvas id="chartTurnsByRating"></canvas>
</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>Rating Bracket</th><th class="num">Avg. Turns</th><th class="num">Median Turns</th><th class="num">Games</th></tr>
  <tr><td>&lt;1,200</td><td class="num">50.6</td><td class="num">48.0</td><td class="num">1,594</td></tr>
